{
  "rationale": "The system scenario is the binding constraint: a 1 us tick dumps 32 requests at once and all 32 must be back within 0.44 us (p99). That means the unit must retire roughly one result per cycle. `iterative` needs N+3 >= 7 cycles/result and `unrolled_k` needs ceil(N/k)+3 >= 4 cycles/result, so 32 results cost >= 128-224 cycles; even at an optimistic 200-300 MHz that is 0.6-1.1 us, and their Fmax is far below that for large k/N. They also miss the 32 Msps throughput floor. So only the two fully-pipelined families (1 result/cycle) can plausibly satisfy the spec, and I spend the whole budget on them. Accuracy 2^-12 (output LSB = 2^-(W-2)) forces W >= ~15 and N >= ~13; the area objective with HV ref 4000 means the useful front runs from the minimum-area feasible corner (small W/N, minimal guards) up to ~4000 LUT+FF, so I keep W and N wide enough to reach that corner and to expose the accuracy/area knee. `pipelined_m` should win on FFs (registers every m stages instead of every stage) but loses Fmax, which directly trades against the batch-latency bound, so both families must be mapped. angle_guard/frac_guard are cheap accuracy knobs and are included on both.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Fully pipelined, 1 result/cycle, best Fmax of the two feasible families -> most margin on sys_p99_batch_us (latency N+2 plus 31 drain cycles). This is the family most likely to survive the L2 re-simulation, and it anchors the high-accuracy / high-area end of the front. W and N ranges cover the 2^-12 feasibility boundary up to ~20 accuracy bits; guards and rounding are the fine accuracy/area knobs.",
